Ansible Automation Platform (AAP) with Splunk:
​Key Steps for Splunk Log Aggregation Setup
​Navigation
​Go to Settings in the Ansible Automation Platform UI.
​Under Automation Execution, select Logging.
​Configuration
​Aggregator Details: Enter the Splunk logging aggregator endpoint/details provided by the Splunk team.
​Authentication: Input the encrypted logging aggregator token or password obtained from Splunk.
​Activation & Functionality
​Enable External Logging: Toggle Enable External Logging to ON to initiate the log stream.
​Log Forwarding: Once enabled, AAP automatically forwards all platform logs to Splunk continuously.
​Maintenance: This is a one-time configuration; no manual daily intervention is required once active.






Access Control & Permissions for Splunk Integration Settings
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Permissions in Ansible Automation Platform (AAP) are strictly tied to specific roles, groups, and user privilege levels.
Super User vs. Standard Administrator Privileges
Super User (admin): Has full privileges across AAP, including the exclusive ability to view, configure, or modify external logging settings (Splunk integration) under Settings > Automation Execution > Logging.
Standard Administrator (e.g., Dikshita): Even when assigned to the Administrator group, regular administrators do not see the Logging option under Automation Execution, restricting them from modifying logging configurations.
Granular Privilege Distribution
Access scales down strictly across user roles: Super User \rightarrow Administrator \rightarrow Auditor \rightarrow Developer / Operator.
Non-super users are locked out from sensitive system-wide settings like Splunk log forwarding to ensure security and prevent unauthorized changes.




